{
 "translation": "In Chiniot, a fat Muslim madman, who had been an enthusiastic member of the Muslim League and used to bathe fifteen or sixteen times a day, suddenly gave up this habit. His name was Muhammad Ali, so one day he declared in his cell that he was Qaid-e-Azam Muhammad Ali Jinnah. Seeing this, a Sikh madman became Master Tara Singh. It was close to a bloody fight in that cell, but both were declared dangerous madmen and locked up separately. There was a young Hindu lawyer from Lahore who had gone mad after failing in love. When he heard that Amritsar had gone to Hindustan, he was very sad. He had fallen in love with a Hindu girl in that same city, although that girl had rejected him. But even in his madness, he had not forgotten her. Consequently, he used to curse all those Hindu-Muslim leaders who had conspired together to divide Hindustan into two pieces. His beloved became Hindustani and he became Pakistani. When the talk of exchange started, many lunatics explained to the lawyer that he should not lose heart; he would be sent to Hindustan, to that Hindustan where his beloved lived. But he did not want to leave Lahore because he thought that his practice would not run in Amritsar. In the European ward, there were two Anglo-Indian lunatics. When they learned that the British had left after freeing Hindustan, they were very sad. They would secretly spend hours discussing this important problem: what would their status be in the asylum now? Would the European ward remain or would it be abolished? Would they get breakfast or not? Would they not have to eat bloody Indian chapatis instead of double roti? There was a Sikh who had been in the asylum for fifteen years. From his mouth, these strange words were heard all the time: 'O pad di gurgur di anekas di bedhyana di mung di dal of di lantern.' He would neither sleep during the day nor at night. The guards' statement was that in fifteen long years, he had not slept even for a moment. He wouldn't even lie down. However, sometimes he would lean against a wall.",
